      {
        "id": "p62-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta=2\\operatorname{cosec}2\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta=\\frac1{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}=2\\operatorname{cosec}2\\theta.$$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta&=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}+\\frac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\\\\\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta&=\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}\\\\\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta&=\\frac1{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}\\\\\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta&=\\frac2{\\sin2\\theta}\\\\\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta&=2\\operatorname{cosec}2\\theta.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p62-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, solve $\\tan x+\\cot x=8\\cos2x$ for $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$x=\\frac\\pi{24},\\ \\frac{5\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{13\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{17\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{25\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{29\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{37\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{41\\pi}{24}.$$\n\nUse part (a) and the sine double-angle formula to obtain $\\sin4x=\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "Using part (a):\n$$\\begin{align*}2\\operatorname{cosec}2x&=8\\cos2x\\\\\\frac2{\\sin2x}&=8\\cos2x\\\\2&=8\\sin2x\\cos2x\\\\2&=4\\sin4x\\\\\\sin4x&=\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $0\\le4x\\le8\\pi$,\n$$4x=\\frac\\pi6+2k\\pi\\quad\\text{or}\\quad4x=\\frac{5\\pi}6+2k\\pi,\\qquad k=0,1,2,3.$$\nTherefore\n$$\\begin{aligned}x={}&\\frac\\pi{24},\\ \\frac{5\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{13\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{17\\pi}{24},\\\\&\\frac{25\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{29\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{37\\pi}{24},\\ \\frac{41\\pi}{24}.\\end{aligned}$$\nAll eight values have $\\sin x\\ne0$ and $\\cos x\\ne0$, so the original equation is defined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 104
      }
    ]
  },
